      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-08-14T02:50:39Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Why not just have a single opaque \"value []byte\", instead of a pair of values?\n\nThis formulation will make each implementation deal with two values, where instead we could have just have this package encode both into a single buffer, and simplify all implementations of this interface (including the DirCache below).",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 21,
        "startChar": 19,
        "endLine": 21,
        "endChar": 41
      },
      "revId": "0c3b50398588ef127faada1e8521bc5bf192a26a",
      "serverId": "62eb7196-b449-3ce5-99f1-c037f21e1705",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "3ee76958_80c7b63f",
        "filename": "acme/autocert/cache.go",
        "patchSetId": 13
      },
      "lineNbr": 21,
      "author": {
        "id": 6347
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-08-14T11:40:40Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "It would require for the Manager to use some intermediate encoding (e.g. PEM or Go\u0027s gob, or custom) to be able to split the value back into a pair. Is that what you mean?",
      "parentUuid": "3e3be95b_8f4f0192",
      "revId": "0c3b50398588ef127faada1e8521bc5bf192a26a",
      "serverId": "62eb7196-b449-3ce5-99f1-c037f21e1705",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "3e3be95b_5a11b5ab",
        "filename": "acme/autocert/cache.go",
        "patchSetId": 13
      },
      "lineNbr": 21,
      "author": {
        "id": 7555
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-08-15T03:33:47Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yep, pretty much. AFAICT, we don\u0027t really gain anything from having the data split into two pieces, and it complicates the cache implementations. I imagine it\u0027d always be slightly trickier to implement a cache API for a pair of values on top of most kv-stores, rather than just a single value.\n\nConsider using json (or some non-binary encoding), so that clients who use the DirCache without an encrypting wrapper will be able to inspect what\u0027s being stored.\n\nIf you don\u0027t want to encode, another option is to just have cacheGet() just call Get/Set twice with the two values under different keys (maybe \"pub:foo.com\", and \"crt:foo.com\"). But I think encoding is better (for caches that are making RPCs to remote services).",
      "parentUuid": "3ee76958_80c7b63f",
      "revId": "0c3b50398588ef127faada1e8521bc5bf192a26a",
      "serverId": "62eb7196-b449-3ce5-99f1-c037f21e1705",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "3e3be95b_2f4cb59b",
        "filename": "acme/autocert/cache.go",
        "patchSetId": 13
      },
      "lineNbr": 64,
      "author": {
        "id": 7555
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-08-14T02:50:39Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This feels racy if two binaries are pointed at the same directory. Consider writing to a tempfile and renaming into place.\n\nWith the current thing, I think its possible to end up with a file with the concatenation of both keys, where with renaming, you\u0027ll end up with exactly one of them.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 64,
        "startChar": 11,
        "endLine": 64,
        "endChar": 27
      },
      "revId": "0c3b50398588ef127faada1e8521bc5bf192a26a",
