    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "3e0049b2_b8b2f9aa",
        "filename": "acme/autocert/man.go",
        "patchSetId": 13
      },
      "lineNbr": 74,
      "author": {
        "id": 6347
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-08-14T11:40:40Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "sounds good. done.",
      "parentUuid": "3e3be95b_6f1abdcf",
      "revId": "0c3b50398588ef127faada1e8521bc5bf192a26a",
      "serverId": "62eb7196-b449-3ce5-99f1-c037f21e1705",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "3e3be95b_af376501",
        "filename": "acme/autocert/man.go",
        "patchSetId": 13
      },
      "lineNbr": 121,
      "author": {
        "id": 7555
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-08-14T02:50:39Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Shouldn\u0027t this method be checking m.certState first? To avoid deserializing the cert repeatedly during the happy path.\n\nI imagine the overall flow should be something like:\na) check in memory certs, if its there, block on verification completing, then return it.\nb) try and load from the cache; if found, populate the in-memory certs, and return it.\nc) check in memory certs again, if its there, block on verification completing and return it. If its not there, create it, and kickoff acme.",
      "revId": "0c3b50398588ef127faada1e8521bc5bf192a26a",
      "serverId": "62eb7196-b449-3ce5-99f1-c037f21e1705",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "befa79f0_5cdbdd16",
        "filename": "acme/autocert/man.go",
        "patchSetId": 13
      },
      "lineNbr": 121,
      "author": {
        "id": 6347
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-08-14T11:40:40Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "It\u0027s a good point but how would you handle a case where in-memory cert is expired or revoked (either by the user or CA). Well, expired is probably easy, but not the rest, where a cert state is changed externally.\n\nThe current Manager implementation is weak on renewal side. I would expect a future improved version kick off a goroutine which would renew the certs preemptively. It could also then re-populate in-memory certs safely.",
      "parentUuid": "3e3be95b_af376501",
      "revId": "0c3b50398588ef127faada1e8521bc5bf192a26a",
      "serverId": "62eb7196-b449-3ce5-99f1-c037f21e1705",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "3e3be95b_1a724d14",
        "filename": "acme/autocert/man.go",
        "patchSetId": 13
      },
      "lineNbr": 121,
      "author": {
        "id": 7555
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-08-15T03:33:47Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I see, you\u0027re thinking of a case where another instance of the Manager renews (or otherwise updates) the cache. Interesting.\n\nI still think we shouldn\u0027t pay the cost of parsing the cert on every connection creation (even  worse if the Cache implementation the client is using goes to the disk or network).\n\nThere\u0027s probably some compromise like having a background goroutine that periodically syncs the cache state for recently accessed domains with the in-memory map, but this is an implementation detail that can probably wait for a different CL.",
      "parentUuid": "befa79f0_5cdbdd16",
      "revId": "0c3b50398588ef127faada1e8521bc5bf192a26a",
      "serverId": "62eb7196-b449-3ce5-99f1-c037f21e1705",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "3e3be95b_6f52bd3e",
        "filename": "acme/autocert/man.go",
        "patchSetId": 13
      },
      "lineNbr": 241,
      "author": {
        "id": 7555
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-08-14T02:50:39Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Out of curiosity, is there any reason to default to rsa? Not an expert, but my understanding is ECC certs are 10x smaller with equivalent (or higher) security levels.",
      "revId": "0c3b50398588ef127faada1e8521bc5bf192a26a",
      "serverId": "62eb7196-b449-3ce5-99f1-c037f21e1705",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "3e0049b2_f8bc7180",
        "filename": "acme/autocert/man.go",
        "patchSetId": 13
      },
      "lineNbr": 241,
      "author": {
        "id": 6347
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-08-14T11:40:40Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "True. Historically though, Let\u0027s Encrypt only supported RSA: https://community.letsencrypt.org/t/elliptic-curve-cryptography-ecc-support/34\nPlus, I was afraid ECC certs are not as widely supported, yet.\n\nIdeally, I\u0027d love for the future Manager to have a key generator hook so that users can decide for themselves.",
      "parentUuid": "3e3be95b_6f52bd3e",
      "revId": "0c3b50398588ef127faada1e8521bc5bf192a26a",
      "serverId": "62eb7196-b449-3ce5-99f1-c037f21e1705",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "fef4f103_fe28e1ea",
        "filename": "acme/autocert/man.go",
        "patchSetId": 13
      },
      "lineNbr": 241,
      "author": {
        "id": 14880
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-08-14T13:55:28Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "ECC certs are widely supported, for example, rsc.io uses ECC certs and ssllabs.com reports wide support[0]. If you are worried about clients without ECC support, you can detect that in the ClientHelloInfo and serve a RSA cert, that way clients that support ECC reap the benefits (faster validation, less bytes on the wire) while continuing to support the rare clients that can talk to TLS 1.0 but don\u0027t support ECC.\n\n[0]: https://www.ssllabs.com/ssltest/analyze.html?d\u003drsc.io",
      "parentUuid": "3e0049b2_f8bc7180",
      "revId": "0c3b50398588ef127faada1e8521bc5bf192a26a",
      "serverId": "62eb7196-b449-3ce5-99f1-c037f21e1705",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "def1ed14_dd8febd2",
        "filename": "acme/autocert/man.go",
        "patchSetId": 13
      },
      "lineNbr": 241,
      "author": {
        "id": 6347
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-08-14T14:54:39Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yes, I\u0027m worried about the clients. Detecting the support in ClientHelloInfo will be too late. Unless you\u0027re alluding to 2 certs per domain, presenting one or the other based on the client. Either way, I think it could be added in the next round of changes, once this is submitted.",
      "parentUuid": "fef4f103_fe28e1ea",
      "revId": "0c3b50398588ef127faada1e8521bc5bf192a26a",
      "serverId": "62eb7196-b449-3ce5-99f1-c037f21e1705",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
